# Work sessions

This folder is the user's work root. It is synced to OneDrive and is the source of the
audit trail, so its layout matters.

## One folder per request

**Never write files into the root of this folder.** Each piece of work lives in
`YYYY/MM/DD/HH-mm-ss_short-slug/` (local time, lowercase, dashes, no colons). If a session is
running in the root, create the request folder first and announce it. A clearly separate piece
of work later in the same session gets its own folder.

Folders starting with `_` are infrastructure (`_config/` settings and the standing context
below, `_audit/` exports, ledger and maps), not requests.

## How a session starts

`claude-new -t TICKET "topic"` creates the folder, records the profile, the **Jira ticket**
(`PREFIX-123` such as `{{CWS_TICKET_EXAMPLE}}`, or `Other`) and the **task type** in
`.session.json`, and launches Claude there. With a ticket that already has a recent folder it
offers to continue in it. `-n` keeps the session out of the audit and the weekly review.

The ticket puts the session on the right tracker row in the weekly review. If a request has no
ticket and the work clearly belongs to one, say so.

The task type is what **triggered** the work, not what it touched on the way: a job error that
leads to a permission change is still `job errors`. If the trigger turns out to be something
else, run `claude-type "<type>"` and say so in one line.

## Who is the ball with?

When a session ends with someone else owing an answer, run
`claude-wait them "<who>" "<what for>" --where teams|mail|jira [--link URL] --note "<where things stand>"`
in the folder and say so in one line; `claude-wait me ...` when the user owes the reply;
`claude-wait none` when it is settled. The note is for someone who was not here: what was
asked, what was answered and where, what is open and by when. It goes to the folder's
`thread.md`. **If a folder has a `thread.md`, read it before doing anything else there.**

## Finding and reviewing

- `claude-search QUERY [--ai]` finds past sessions on a ticket or topic. Use it when the user
  asks where or when they worked on something; do not grep transcripts.
- `claude-audit [--day|--week|--month]` reconstructs the trail from the transcripts. The
  `weekly-review` skill fills the tracker; its decisions live in `_audit/review/ledger.json`,
  changed only through `claude-audit --apply`.

